# `viola-core` BACKLOG

Designed-but-deferred entries for `viola-core`. Items here are decisions made; they ship in the slice that first needs them.

## InvocationSurface

Restore as a new `Resource<InvocationSurface>` when a real use case surfaces. The Slice 1 SRC CL hinted at an Lsp-initiated run egressing through a Hook, but no current code path needs the distinction. Re-introducing the distinction is a public-layout change to `Workspace`; pre-1.0 absorbs the churn, but consumers that pin Workspace by-field will need an update.

## Runtime test-Ctx fixture for LoadConfig

Slice 2b shipped a compile-time AccessSet-shape witness test for `LoadConfig` but deferred the runtime parse-success and parse-failure tests. The Slice 1 WuCtxStub Stub provider calls unimplemented on every API method; runtime tests need a real test-Ctx fixture backing each Resource and Column with concrete storage. The fixture lives at viola-core/tests/load_config.rs and is itself non-trivial: it implements every Has accessor trait on a holder type that owns `ConfigBytes`, `ViolaCfg`, and a `Column<WuDiagnostic>` buffer. Lands per a separate body-and-fixture PR before Slice 3 opens to keep the test posture honest.

## Host-shim long-lived interner wiring

The public hilavitkutin-str Str surface ships only `__make` and `__runtime` constructors keyed by `Bits<28, Hot>` ids. Slice 2b's parse-failure path in LoadConfig uses `Str::default()` as a placeholder until the host shim registers a long-lived interner at scheduler-builder time. The interner outlives the scheduler run; the host shim's contract is that resource fields holding `Str` reference interned bytes valid for the duration of the run. Slice 3's LoadPlugins body inherits the same gap for the `PluginEntry.name` field. Wiring lands per a follow-up DESIGN section that names the host-shim interner contract concretely (storage lifetime, capacity caps, the registration mechanism on Scheduler::builder).

## Column::len writer-side accessor in hilavitkutin-api

The hilavitkutin-api `ColumnWriterApi` surface today exposes `write(&self, i: USize, v: T)` keyed by an explicit slot index; there is no `len(&self) -> USize` accessor on the writer side. Slice 3's `LoadPlugins::execute` works around the gap by maintaining a local counter (`loaded_count`) that tracks the column length by construction: the column starts empty per run, `LoadPlugins` is the sole writer per the AccessSet contract, and the counter increments only on a successful push. The `WuDiagnostic` failure column gets the same treatment with a sibling `failure_count`. Future hilavitkutin-api revision adds `Column::len` on the writer side; once it lands, the workaround simplifies and the local counters drop.

## Runtime test-Ctx fixture for LoadPlugins

Slice 3 shipped a compile-time AccessSet-shape witness test for `LoadPlugins` but deferred the runtime parse-success and dlopen-failure tests. Inherits the same blocker as the LoadConfig fixture above: WuCtxStub Stub provider calls unimplemented on every API method. The LoadPlugins fixture is more involved than LoadConfig's: it needs concrete storage for `Resource<ViolaCfg>`, `Resource<ExtensionHost>`, `Column<PluginEntry>`, and `Column<WuDiagnostic>` simultaneously, plus a strategy for the dlopen side (real `Library::load` against a workspace-local fixture cdylib, or a mockable indirection). Lands per a separate body-and-fixture PR after the LoadConfig fixture proves the shape; the LoadPlugins fixture reuses LoadConfig's `Has`-accessor scaffolding.

## Filesystem walker for no_std host

Slice 4 picks Path B (host shim walks at scheduler-builder time, WU projects). Path A (in-WU walker via libc-syscalls) is the eventual right shape once a hilavitkutin-fs stack-side helper exists. The walker would mirror the `hilavitkutin_linking` pattern: wrap libc opendir / readdir / closedir without alloc, expose a Cap-bounded iteration surface, integrate with hilavitkutin-persistence cold-store for incremental walks. Open design questions (error semantics, symlink loop detection, max-depth caps) defer until the stack-side conversation opens. Once it lands, the DiscoverFiles body shifts from reading `Resource<DiscoveredFilePaths>` to in-WU walking; the host shim drops its filesystem-walking responsibility.

## Include exclude pattern matching for DiscoverFiles

The scoping memo names include / exclude pattern matching as a DiscoverFiles responsibility. `viola_config::ViolaConfigOwned` today carries no `includes` or `excludes` field; the patterns themselves need a viola-config round that designs the `IncludePattern` and `ExcludePattern` newtypes, the array caps, the populate-arm fill-in, and the matching semantics (glob, prefix, exact). The DiscoverFiles body refinement waits for that round. When it lands, DiscoverFiles AccessSet extends: Read gains `Resource<ViolaCfg>`, Write gains `Column<WuDiagnostic>` for invalid-pattern errors. Today's Slice 4 body unconditionally projects every input slot.

## FileKind classification beyond Regular

Slice 4 constructs only `FileKind::Regular` on every projection because the host shim today reports only path existence. Refinement: extend `Resource<DiscoveredFilePaths>` to carry per-path kind classification (sibling `Cell<[FileKind; MAX_DISCOVERED_FILES]>` array, or a packed bitfield, or a sibling `Resource<FileKinds>`) populated by the host shim at scheduler-builder time. Once available, the body reads the per-entry kind and constructs the matching `FileInfo` variant.

## FileInfo size hints

`FileInfo` ships two fields in Slice 4: path and kind. Future Slice 6 (RunLint) may want file-size hints for buffer pre-sizing and dispatch prioritisation. Tracks the third field shape as a future scoping decision: a `size: arvo::USize` field, a per-Kind enum payload, or a sibling `Column<FileSize>`. The right shape depends on RunLint specifics.

## Workspace-relative diagnostic context for DiscoverFiles

Slice 4 narrows the AccessSet to consume only `Resource<DiscoveredFilePaths>`; `Resource<Workspace>` drops from the Slice 1 forward-looking Read. When a future producer slice extends DiscoverFiles to emit `WuDiagnostic` records (e.g. invalid-pattern errors after the include / exclude refinement, or kind-classification errors), the workspace root may want to anchor diagnostic messages. That refinement extends Read to include `Resource<Workspace>` at the point of consumption. Tracked here as the trigger.

## Runtime test-Ctx fixture for DiscoverFiles

Slice 4 ships a compile-time AccessSet-shape witness test for DiscoverFiles. Runtime projection tests defer to a follow-up that shares fixture infrastructure with the Slice 2b LoadConfig and Slice 3 LoadPlugins runtime fixtures. The DiscoverFiles fixture needs concrete storage for `Resource<DiscoveredFilePaths>` and `Column<FileInfo>` at minimum; refinement extensions add more Resources/Columns as their AccessSet extensions land.

## RunRunner body and FFI invocation

Shipped in Slice 5b. The body resolves the runner plugin from `Resource<ExtensionHost>` plus `Column<PluginEntry>` (finds the entry with the runner-role bit set, resolves its `&Library`, extracts the runner vtable from the descriptor via `runner_vtable_from_library`), marshals `RunScope` from Resources at the FFI boundary, calls `execute_scope`, writes the returned `NamPayload` into `Column<Nam>` at slot zero. On failure (descriptor missing, vtable absent, AbiStatus != Ok), writes `WuDiagnostic { source: WuDiagnosticSource::RunRunner }` to `Column<WuDiagnostic>`. AccessSet extended per the Slice 5b DOC CL. Initial body ships with placeholders for workspace_root, surface, host_ctx, and FileEntry enrichment fields per the four still-deferred Slice 5a BACKLOG entries below.

## FileEntryBuffer Resource for RunRunner

Shipped in Slice 5b. The Resource shape lands in viola-core: `UnsafeCell<[FileEntry; MAX_DISCOVERED_FILES]>` plus `Cell<arvo::USize>` length, four-invariant Sync contract mirroring DiscoveredFilePaths, `pub unsafe fn push` host-shim seam, safe `entries_len` and `entries_ptr` read accessors. The host-shim implementation that actually populates the Resource lands in Slice 8 (viola-cli host-shim rewire); the Resource shape ships now so the WU can consume it.

## FileEntry enrichment for RunScope

`viola_plugin_abi::FileEntry` carries `path: BytesRef`, `language: BytesRef`, `hash: BytesRef`, `size_bytes: u64` beyond the path the host shim discovers. Slice 5b initial ship sends empties for language and hash, zero for size_bytes; subsequent refinement adds extension-driven language detection, SHA-256 hash computation at scheduler-builder time, and stat-based size_bytes population. Each enrichment lands as a host-shim-side change; the viola-core Resource shape stays stable.

## runner_vtable_from_library helper for viola-core invoke

The helper shipped in Slice 5b: `runner_vtable_from_library(&hilavitkutin_linking::Library) -> Maybe<&'static RunnerExecuteScopeVtable>` walks the descriptor's provider table per the Slice 3 `resolve_descriptor` pattern. The pre-#254 `runner_vtable(&Extension)` stays until Slice 8 deletes the `Extension` type. The sibling `lint_vtable_from_library` for Slice 6 stays a forward-looking note and lands in that round.

## host_ctx model for FFI plugin callbacks

The FFI signature passes `host_ctx: *mut c_void` opaquely; the plugin uses it to call back into the host (logging, IPC, diagnostic emission per cdylib boundary memo Shape D). Slice 5b passes `core::ptr::null_mut()`; plugins must tolerate null per the ABI documentation. Concrete trigger for the real model: Slice 8 host-shim wire-up exposes the natural call site for callback registration; the follow-up adds `Resource<HostCallbackTable>` carrying the callback vtable and threads its pointer through the WU body. Until then null is the documented contract.

## Resource<RunSurface> and CiState surface field

`RunScope.surface: RunSurface` enum (Cli, Lsp, etc.) needs a host-shim-picked value. `Resource<CiState>` carries `is_ci` but not `surface`. Slice 5b either hardcodes `RunSurface::Cli` or introduces a new `Resource<RunSurface>` populated by the host shim at scheduler-builder time. The choice waits on viola-cli host-shim shape; recommended path is the new Resource because future LSP integration (Slice 7+) will also need surface awareness.

## Workspace path Str-to-bytes resolution for FFI

`RunScope.workspace_root: BytesRef` is a raw bytes pointer. `Resource<Workspace>.path: Str` is an interned handle. Resolving the handle back to raw bytes requires the host-shim long-lived interner (already tracked in the Slice 2b "Host-shim long-lived interner wiring" BACKLOG entry). Slice 5b ships `BytesRef::EMPTY` for workspace_root until the interner lands; downstream plugins tolerate empty workspace_root per the ABI.
